{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2521 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c026b347bae73a770d327cdb505150bcacb52f10142f2f7bad79fd6e625f4cb1" :edb/db-before-t 2520 :edb/db-after-t 2521 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535833 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:26:32.304Z" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128946 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/1d180fd5de88ebc465df522879142e5a4c5446c758aaccde792092968078c254.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x=2y^3-2y$ and  $x=y^3-4y,$ and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm{d}A.$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128946 1100 "The region $D$ is enclosed by the curves $x=2y^3-2y$ and $x=y^3-4y$, and the $y$-axis, as shown in the picture. Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm{d}A$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/ad479776abe1b64937bf9c79c413a827.png)" 13194139535833 false] [17592186128946 1145 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions $D_1$ and $D_2$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/f0e34c7f4bb3ec1fc5266af37fe73361.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq y \\leq 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^3\\!-\\!4y \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 2y^3\\!-\\!2y \\leq x \\leq 0 \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D_{1})dA + ∬_(D_{2})dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{0}∫_{0}^{y^{3} - 4y}dxdy + ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{2y^{3} - 2y}^{0}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{0}[x]_{0}^{y^{3} - 4y}dy + ∫_{0}^{1}[x]_{2y^{3} - 2y}^{0}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{0}(y^{3} - 4y)dy + ∫_{0}^{1}(2y - 2y^{3})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{4}}{4} - 2y^{2}]_{-2}^{0} + [y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 4 + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 false] [17592186128946 1145 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/6812e8036df171e162bee49d2edcff6d4a742e7bb4f14247713bd2332c64ccc6.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq y  \\leq 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^3\\!-\\!4y \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nD_2 = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y  \\leq 1, \\quad 2y^3\\!-\\!2y \\leq x \\leq 0 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}-4y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{3}-2y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{3}-4y}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{2{y}^{3}-2y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{3}-4y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}-2{y}^{2}]}_{-2}^{0}+{[{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128947 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curves $y= 2^x$, $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and lines $x=0$ and $y=2$, as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/44c00a18090dd9acfb716d4de0839aed.png)" 13194139535833 false] [17592186128947 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curves $y= 2^x,$ $y=\\sqrt{x}$  and lines $x=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/b80527fdef6f890581b8f56431e1b72a8f7eae3b41827caa16486ed9f2fd45ea.png)" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128947 1145 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions $D_1$ and $D_2$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/dfed9d77548799c30e813d5415b96807.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt x \\leq y \\leq 2^x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\quad \\sqrt x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D_{1})f(x, y)dA + ∬_(D_{2})f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2^{x}}f(x, y)dydx + ∫_{1}^{4}∫_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x, y)dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 false] [17592186128947 1145 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as shown below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/939e46b3ed4266f3c18d3567f951276e420292781cfc414711c244f453bc4029.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x  \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt x \\leq y \\leq 2^x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x  \\leq 4, \\quad \\sqrt x \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{{2}^{x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128948 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y=2+\\ln x$, and the lines $y=1$, $x=1$, and $x=e$, as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/90badcdff8d1723816783ecee432d5a9.png)" 13194139535833 false] [17592186128948 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y=2+\\ln x,$  and the lines $y=1$, $x=1,$ and  $x=e,$  as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/4b0dff15fa9fa93ed61183702bff6bb443daf14b0adbf56cf4b97e3bd830ceea.png)" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128948 1145 "First, notice that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions $D_1$ and $D_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/2228ad860fea1e2c30d20bce749f654da02a97c65fd5e70152a07761e26f3e78.png)\n\n    \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y  \\leq 2, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq e \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\nD_2 = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 2 \\leq y  \\leq 3, \\quad e^{y-2} \\leq x \\leq e\\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-2}}^{e}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186128948 1145 "First, notice that:\n\n$$\ny = 2 + \\ln x \\mid ⟹x = e^{y - 2}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions $D_1$ and $D_2$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/97aa046fce141be1472a9103f7079cae.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 1 \\leq x \\leq e \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 2 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad e^{y-2} \\leq x \\leq e\\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D_{1})f(x, y)dA + ∬_(D_{2})f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{1}^{2}∫_{1}^{e}f(x, y)dxdy + ∫_{2}^{3}∫_{e^{y - 2}}^{e}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 false] [17592186129127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368924 13194139535833 true] [17592186129127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368945 13194139535833 true] [17592186129127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368952 13194139535833 true] [17592186129127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368966 13194139535833 true] [17592186129127 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368973 13194139535833 true] [17592186129128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368882 13194139535833 true] [17592186129128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368889 13194139535833 true] [17592186129128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368896 13194139535833 true] [17592186129128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368931 13194139535833 true] [17592186129128 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368959 13194139535833 true] [17592186129129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368903 13194139535833 true] [17592186129129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368910 13194139535833 true] [17592186129129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368917 13194139535833 true] [17592186129129 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368938 13194139535833 true] [17592186129553 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535833 true] [17592186129553 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/3fb6039f0a1de69d41644d7153d205062e85cdfce7cdb6b6270d952f2dbab6c3.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=x+6,$ $y=2x,$ and $y=3x,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139535833 true] [17592186129553 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1996/Source/Images/q-37757.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y = x + 6$, $y = 2x$, and $y = 3x$, as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x, y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." 13194139535833 false] [17592186129553 1145 "First, let's write our equations in terms of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ y=x+6 & \\,⟹\\,x=y-6 \\\\ y=3x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type II regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/c453d46a1f0cd6e6ddfa926de471fe38663df2bd86b9cdf4e58ce8d42462774a.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 9,\\,\\frac{y}{3}\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,9\\le y\\le 12,\\,y-6\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\int }_{y/3}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{9}^{12}{\\int }_{y-6}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368883 13194139535833 true] [17592186368882 1089 "q-132290" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368882 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535833 true] [17592186368882 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/797cddb4ea3cb41959df88e56517e9ee71e76e3cd08a9a93d2957b9792d14abb.png)\n\nConsider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=-\\frac{2x}{3},$ $y=\\frac{4x}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{x}{3}+3,$ as shown in the picture. Express the double integral of the function $f(x,y)$ over the region $D$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." 13194139535833 true] [17592186368882 1113 #uuid "7aeb314a-1c40-56e8-9c79-581f6c42093d" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368882 1145 "The region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/0e69b1cb480b87216273db4336adff57cbf9d9bc450aac4b0cdb58b1ae63f6f7.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{2x}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}+3}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{4x}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{3}+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368884 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368885 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368886 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368887 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368888 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1041 "selection" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368886 13194139535833 true] [17592186368883 1110 #uuid "4ef0fbe2-c555-56be-a2d1-ce9c7e5b30a8" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368884 1034 "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{x/3+3}^{-2x/3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535833 true] [17592186368884 1111 #uuid "508bdf93-8eb5-59be-b79c-263552dd6348" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368885 1034 "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535833 true] [17592186368885 1111 #uuid "1aa0e36f-722b-50e3-a646-719b1dd482f3" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368886 1034 "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{4x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368886 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535833 true] [17592186368886 1111 #uuid "96bd9871-6319-56ee-b0b3-1a1911e4aaa9" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368887 1034 "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{-2x/3}^{x/3+3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x/3+3}^{4x/3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368887 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535833 true] [17592186368887 1111 #uuid "0a440729-dc6c-5993-88e4-f68d2c02c029" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368888 1034 "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{x/3+3}^{-2x/3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{x/3+3}^{4x/3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535833 true] [17592186368888 1111 #uuid "41358bee-8b70-56cf-847c-630f37ddbc72" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368890 13194139535833 true] [17592186368889 1089 "q-132291" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535833 true] [17592186368889 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $y=-x,$ $y=2$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Express ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type I regions." 13194139535833 true] [17592186368889 1113 #uuid "b9666ae8-02f3-5bcc-8118-4503675d1849" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368889 1145 "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/990f2180e92ffa9ae0b77a7c8c4aae284cacf03f7a7a62b1ed0b56f24705d671.png)\n\nThe region $D$ can be partitioned into two type I regions ${D}_{1}$ and ${D}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070962/assets/db7fe0aa7b746eab86c011545ec37d59d8f225f02a9fc6f4388754e1c528db8c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-x\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the additivity property of double integrals, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,dA+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,dA \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368891 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368892 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368893 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368894 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368895 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1041 "selection" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368891 13194139535833 true] [17592186368890 1110 #uuid "3fba3a7a-123d-57b2-bdcd-2020283f8a59" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368891 1034 "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx" 13194139535833 true] [17592186368891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535833 true] [17592186368891 1111 #uuid "525fd8e3-926f-5961-882f-ea53f19a4fa0" 13194139535833 true] [1759